topics/types-of-indeterminate-forms.content:
\begin{align}
&amp;\dfrac{0}{0}\\[6pt]
&amp;\dfrac{\infty}{\infty}\\[6pt]
&amp;0\times\infty\\[6pt]
&amp;\infty-\infty\\[6pt]
&amp;1^\infty\\[6pt]
&amp;0^0\\[6pt]
&amp;\infty^{0}
\end{align}
Example:
Let f(x) = 2x and g(x) = 3x
Consider h(x)=\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}
We can’t calculate h(0), it is undefined, but we can calculate \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 0}}\  h(x)
The point is we can’t calculate \dfrac{0}{0} but if the limit of a function is tending towards this calculation, we may be able to evaluate that limit.
In this case \displaystyle{\lim_{x \to 0}}\  h(x) = \dfrac{2}{3}
but if f(x)=5x, the limit would have been \dfrac{5}{3} showing that just knowing that the limit was tending towards \dfrac{0}{0} was insufficient knowledge to deduce the limit.
